Q: Idiomatic rust for sorting a pair of integers (a,b) such that a &lt; b A: <p>Maybe so? For readability:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>fn swap_unless&lt;T&gt;(c: bool, x: &amp;mut T, y: &amp;mut T) { if !c { std::mem::swap(x, y) }}

swap_unless(a &lt; b, &amp;mut a, &amp;mut b);
</code></pre>

Q: Can&#39;t use properties and methods from the object passed as an argument to the Phaser event handler function A: <p>The issue is that you are using the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Spread_syntax" rel="nofollow noreferrer">spread operator</a> on a parameter, that is not needed. All passed passed parameters are now stuffed into a an array.<br />
And you are emitting an Object. So you could remove the spread operator, or select the first item in the <code>args</code> variable, I would opt for removing the spread operator. <em>(As @kikom also menitoned in the comments)</em></p>
<p>Just remove the <code>...</code>, in this line and it works:</p>
<pre><code>const testEventListener = (args: any[]) =&gt; {
    // ...
};
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Update:</strong></p>
<p><strong>btw.:</strong> <em>you are using the spread operator way too much in your code, just use it when you really need it, since on each nested call the arguments will be stuffed in a another Array, and you are create unnecessary depth.</em></p>
